Midland did the opposite of what the law requires. Instead of notifying me first, they skipped the validation process completely and immediately reported the account. Reporting a debt that was never validated constitutes furnishing information that is not known to be accurate, which violates FDCPA 1692e ( 8 ) ( communicating credit information knownor that should be knownto be false or unverified ). It also violates FCRA 623 ( a ) ( 1 ) ( A ) because a furnisher must ensure accuracy before reporting anything to the credit bureaus. 
Additionally, the CFPB has taken multiple enforcement actions specifically against Midland Credit Management for failing to send validation notices, including actions in XXXX, XXXX and XXXX, where the CFPB found systemic violations of 1692g ( a ) and ordered Midland to stop reporting unverified debts. Midlands continued conduct here matches the same behaviors criticized and penalized in those enforcement cases.

I learned about this account only from reviewing my credit reportnot from any notice, letter, or contact by Midland. Because the legally required initial communication was never sent, I was denied my federal right to dispute the debt and request validation before any credit reporting occurred. Midland bypassed the core consumer protection that 1692g ( a ) is designed to provide. 
This account was furnished to the bureaus illegally, without the mandatory disclosure, and without giving me the chance to question or verify it. That is a clear violation of federal law.",,ENCORE CAPITAL GROUP INC.,GA,XXXXX,,Web,2025-11-19,Closed with explanation,Yes
